{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-900eb383fe3fc4bc","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\ttitle: 'Base image compliance - Post-quantum cryptography support '\n4\tstatus: In Progress\n5\tpriority: Normal\n6\tlabels:\n7\t- PQC_Status\n8\t- refined\n9\t- strat-epics-created\n10\tlinks:\n11\t- 'source-rfe: RHAIRFE-7174'\n12\t- 'depends-on: RHAISTRAT-7816'\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## Business Need (from RFE)\n16\t\n17\t> The full business need is maintained in the source RFE: RHAIRFE-7174\n18\t\n19\t## Strategy (AI Generated by Agentic SDLC Pipeline)\n20\t\n21\t### TL;DR\n22\t\n23\tRHAISTRAT-8001 tracks RHOAI's switch to PQC-enabled runtime base images for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 compliance. **Deadline: September 15, 2026** — each component team either completes the base image switch OR files an OCP exception via [OCPEXCEPT]([internal link removed]).\n24\t\n25\tConfirmed available PQC base images (Tobin Pellicort, August 2026):\n26\t\n27\t* `registry.redhat.io/ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` — reports `DEFAULT:PQ`\n28\t* `registry.redhat.io/rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` — reports `DEFAULT:PQ`\n29\t\n30\tPQC enablement is **runtime only** — no special builder image needed. Only the final runtime stage of each image needs to switch. Teams link their tracking tickets to RHAISTRAT-8001 for OCP 5.0 visibility.\n31\t\n32\tGo ML-KEM and TLSAdherence are owned by RHAISTRAT-7816. Post-OCP-5.0 work (micro images, Hummingbird/UBI10) is tracked separately and is not in scope here.\n33\t\n34\t### Technical Approach\n35\t\n36\t#### What the PQC Base Image Does\n37\t\n38\tThe `DEFAULT:PQ` crypto policy in `ubi9-minimal-pqc` and `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` enables PQC algorithms in **system OpenSSL**. Container images do not inherit crypto policy from the host — each image must configure its own. Switching the final runtime stage to a PQC base image is sufficient for components that use system OpenSSL for TLS.\n39\t\n40\tComponents that do **not** use system OpenSSL (rustls+ring, Java/NSS) cannot be remediated by a base image swap alone. Those teams must file OCP exceptions if they cannot meet the September 15 deadline through other means.\n41\t\n42\t#### Scope — All RHOAI Runtime Images\n43\t\n44\tEvery RHOAI component image must either:\n45\t\n46\t1. Switch its final runtime stage to the appropriate PQC base image, **or**\n47\t2. File an OCP exception by September 15, documenting why it cannot comply and providing a remediation plan\n48\t\n49\tThe complete image list for RHOAI 3.5 is at [RHOAI-Build-Config rhoai-3.5 CSV](https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/RHOAI-Build-Config/blob/rhoai-3.5/bundle/manifests/rhods-operator.clusterserviceversion.yaml#L2032-L2338) (153 relatedImages). The organized image inventory with base image and builder information is maintained locally at `3.5-component-images-organized.md`.\n50\t\n51\t**Base image mapping:**\n52\t\n53\t| Current base | Switch to | Notes |\n54\t| --- | --- | --- |\n55\t| `ubi9/ubi-minimal:latest` | `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` | \\~68 images — Go operators, most platform components |\n56\t| `rhel9-6-els/rhel:9.6-*` | `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` | \\~34 images — notebooks, workbenches, training runtimes, mlserver |\n57\t| `ubi9/python-311:latest` | PQC variant TBD | 5 images — training variants, lmes-job |\n58\t| `ubi9/python-312:latest` | PQC variant TBD | 4 images — training variants |\n59\t| `ubi9/python-312-minimal:latest` | PQC variant TBD | 1 image — feature-server |\n60\t| `ubi9/nodejs-22:latest` | PQC variant or exception | 1 image — dashboard (largely behind Go proxy) |\n61\t| `ubi9/openjdk-17-runtime:latest` | Exception expected | TrustyAI Java — NSS backend, not OpenSSL |\n62\t\n63\tPython and nodejs PQC base image variants are unconfirmed — AIPCC spike RHOAIENG-7195 is tracking this.\n64\t\n65\t#### Components Likely Needing OCP Exceptions\n66\t\n67\tTeams owning the following should plan to file exceptions by September 15 if they cannot remediate in time:\n68\t\n69\t* **fms-guardrails-orchestrator** — rustls+ring TLS stack, cannot inherit PQC from base image; TLS migration spike RHOAIENG-7452\n70\t* **TrustyAI explainability service** — Java/NSS, not OpenSSL; JDK LTS timeline (JDK 29, September 2027)\n71\t* **trainer data cache** — Rust TLS posture unknown; audit spike RHOAIENG-7196\n72\t\n73\tEach team files their own exception — template at [OCPEXCEPT]([internal link removed]).\n74\t\n75\t#### OCP Roadmap Context\n76\t\n77\tPost-OCP-5.0 base image evolution (not in scope here):\n78\t\n79\t* OCP 5.1: micro base images\n80\t* OCP 5.3+: UBI10 / Hummingbird images (defaults PQC, but constrained by x86-64 v3 requirement)\n81\t\n82\t#### Certificate Management: Deferred\n83\t\n84\tML-DSA (PQC digital signatures for X.509 certificates) is not in scope. Current mandate is ML-KEM key exchange only.\n85\t\n86\t### Affected Components\n87\t\n88\tAll RHOAI component images — each team owns switching their image(s) or filing an OCP exception. The full list is at [RHOAI-Build-Config rhoai-3.5 CSV](https://github.com/red-hat-data-services/RHOAI-Build-Config/blob/rhoai-3.5/bundle/manifests/rhods-operator.clusterserviceversion.yaml#L2032-L2338).\n89\t\n90\tTeams link their tracking tickets to RHAISTRAT-8001 for OCP 5.0 aggregated visibility.\n91\t\n92\t### Impacted Teams\n93\t\n94\t* **All RHOAI component teams**: Switch final runtime stage to PQC base image by September 15, or file OCP exception\n95\t* **AIPCC team**: Confirm/deliver PQC variants for python-311/312/nodejs base images — RHOAIENG-7195\n96\t* **DevOps/Build team**: Coordinate base image pipeline updates\n97\t\n98\t### High Level Requirements\n99\t\n100\t* **\\[P0\\]** All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image (`ubi9-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, or equivalent) for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0\n101\t* **\\[P0\\]** Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception via [OCPEXCEPT]([internal link removed]) with a remediation plan\n102\t* **\\[P0\\]** PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage of the Dockerfile/Containerfile changes; no builder image change needed\n103\t* **\\[Deferred\\]** PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) — not in scope\n104\t\n105\t### Dependencies\n106\t\n107\t* **\\`ubi9-minimal-pqc\\` and \\`rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal\\`** — confirmed available (Tobin Pellicort, August 2026). OCPSTRAT-7413.\n108\t* **PQC variants of python-311/312/nodejs** — unconfirmed. RHOAIENG-7195 tracking.\n109\t* **AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase** (AIPCC-7258) — gates RHEL9-ELS image adoption.\n110\t* **RHAISTRAT-7816** — owns Go ML-KEM, TLSAdherence; separate from this strategy.\n111\t\n112\t### Non-Functional Requirements\n113\t\n114\t* **Security — PQC algorithm compliance (non-Go components)**: fms-guardrails-orchestrator and any Rust components using pure-Rust TLS stacks must support ML-KEM (FIPS 203) hybrid key exchange after migration. Python and native-tls Rust components inherit this from `ubi9-minimal-pqc`. Source: NIST FIPS 203, OCP PQC mandate.\n115\t* **Backwards compatibility**: PQC hybrid key exchange is negotiated opportunistically — clients that do not support PQC continue to work via classic ECDHE. Source: IETF hybrid key exchange draft.\n116\t* **Availability**: Base image switch and Rust TLS migration must not change HA characteristics. Source: no additional replicas required.\n117\t* **Performance**: ⚠️ **NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT** — No numeric regression threshold set. Reference: Chrome \\~10% maximum TLS handshake time regression; PSASTRAT-7170 is the Red Hat PQC benchmarking outcome. _(Owner: PM + Performance team lead — must be resolved before Sprint 2 ends)_\n118\t\n119\t### Out-of-Scope\n120\t\n121\t* **Go ML-KEM, TLSAdherence, TLS-scanner CI** — owned by RHAISTRAT-7816\n122\t* **Post-OCP-5.0 base image evolution** — micro images (5.1), Hummingbird/UBI10 (5.3+) tracked separately\n123\t* **OCP exception filing for individual teams** — each team files their own via [OCPEXCEPT]([internal link removed])\n124\t* **FIPS + PQC combined support** — incompatible on UBI9; not addressed here\n125\t* **ML-DSA / PQC certificates** — deferred\n126\t* PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA/SLH-DSA via cert-manager) — deferred to a follow-on phase\n127\t* Post-quantum cryptography for model weights or training data at rest — network transport PQC only\n128\t* PQC algorithm selection or configuration by end users — platform-level concern, not user-configurable\n129\t* RHOAI-managed external model providers (MaaS/ExternalProvider) — third-party TLS stacks not under RHOAI control\n130\t* PQC for Kubernetes control plane communications — OCP/Kubernetes platform concern\n131\t* FIPS 140-3 certification of PQC algorithms — RHEL-provided OpenSSL is separately certified\n132\t\n133\t### Acceptance Criteria (Proposed — requires PM/Engineering validation)\n134\t\n135\t1. Given all RHOAI runtime images that use system OpenSSL for TLS (Go operators, Python components, native-tls Rust components) have been rebuilt on PQC-enabled base images,\n136\t\n137\t   when `update-crypto-policies --show` is executed inside any switched container,    then the active crypto policy is `DEFAULT:PQ`,    and when `openssl list -kem-algorithms` is executed,    then ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024 appear in the output,    measured by automated CI scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload.    _Note: This criterion applies to OpenSSL-backed components only. Components using non-OpenSSL TLS stacks (rustls+ring, Java/NSS) must be covered by OCP exception filings (AC-2). On FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — DEFAULT:PQ behavior on FIPS clusters is expected to be non-functional and is not a compliance failure for RHOAI 3.6._\n138\t\n139\t1. By September 15, 2026: every RHOAI component image has either (a) been rebuilt on a PQC-enabled base image, or (b) has a filed and acknowledged OCP exception in OCPEXCEPT with a documented remediation plan that includes the component's TLS stack and reason it cannot negotiate ML-KEM. Zero unaccounted-for images remain.\n140\t\n141\t1. All component team tracking tickets are linked to RHAISTRAT-8001, providing an aggregated compliance view for OCP 5.0.\n142\t\n143\t### Effort Estimate\n144\t\n145\t**S (Small) — coordination and deadline tracking**\n146\t\n147\tThis strategy is a compliance coordination tracker. Implementation work is owned by each component team. Primary effort here is:\n148\t\n149\t* Confirming PQC python/nodejs base image availability (RHOAIENG-7195)\n150\t* Aggregating team compliance status via linked tickets\n151\t* Ensuring exception process is exercised by September 15 for non-compliant components\n152\t\n153\t### Risks\n154\t\n155\t* **PQC variants of python-311/312/nodejs not available in time** — teams using those bases may need OCP exceptions. _Mitigation_: RHOAIENG-7195 spike with AIPCC; escalate if no committed date by early September.\n156\t* **AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase slips** — gates RHEL9-ELS image adoption. _Mitigation_: Monitor AIPCC-7258; teams using rhel9-6-els may need exceptions.\n157\t* **Teams miss September 15 deadline without filing exceptions** — creates untracked compliance gap. _Mitigation_: Remind teams weekly; escalate to leadership per Mara/Devin sync decision.\n158\t\n159\t### Assumptions\n160\t\n161\t* `ubi9-minimal-pqc` and `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` images are available and activate DEFAULT:PQ policy — _confirmed (Tobin Pellicort, August 2026; OCPSTRAT-7413)_\n162\t* Go components get ML-KEM for free via native Go crypto; while the base image switch is not functionally required for ML-KEM, it should be done for platform consistency — _confirmed (Tobin Pellicort, July 2026)_\n163\t* FIPS + PQC on UBI9 are currently incompatible; on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release — this is expected behavior, not a compliance failure — _confirmed (Tobin Pellicort, July 2026)_\n164\t* X25519MLKEM768 hybrid key exchange is backwards-compatible with non-PQC TLS 1.3 clients — _confirmed (IETF hybrid key exchange draft)_\n165\t* PQC-signed certificates are deferred to a follow-on phase — _confirmed (scope decision)_\n166\t\n167\t### Open Questions\n168\t\n169\t* **PQC variants of \\`ubi9/python-311\\`, \\`ubi9/python-312\\`, \\`ubi9/nodejs-22\\`** — unconfirmed. RHOAIENG-7195 tracking with AIPCC.\n170\t* **AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date** — gates RHEL9-ELS image adoption. RHOAIENG-7195.\n171\t* **Which teams will file OCP exceptions vs. complete the switch by September 15?** — each team self-identifies and files via [OCPEXCEPT]([internal link removed]).\n172\t\n173\t### Supporting Documentation\n174\t\n175\t* Source RFE: RHAIRFE-7174\n176\t* Dependency: [RHAISTRAT-7816 — Central TLS Profile consistency](RHAISTRAT-7816) — owns Go ML-KEM testing (RHOAIENG-7334), TLSAdherence, TLS-scanner CI\n177\t* Base image delivery: OCPSTRAT-7413 — tracks `ubi9-minimal-pqc` image availability (RHELMISC-7493)\n178\t* PQC benchmarking: PSASTRAT-7170 — Red Hat PQC performance benchmarking outcome\n179\t* Architecture context: fms-guardrails-orchestrator.md — rustls+ring TLS stack, confirmed non-FIPS-validated\n180\t* Architecture context: PLATFORM.md — FIPS posture, component runtime summary\n181\t* NIST FIPS 203 (ML-KEM): [https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final](https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/fips/203/final)\n182\t* Design doc: \\_To be created — PQC Readiness Matrix per component\\_\n183\t\n184\t### Prerequisites & Process Gates\n185\t\n186\t* **Architecture Review**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n187\t* **ODH/RHOAI Build Onboarding**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n188\t* **Licence Validation**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n189\t* **Accelerator/Package Support**: \\_Not applicable — platform crypto concern, no accelerator dependency\\_\n190\t* **Documentation Support**: \\_Pending human review\\_\n191\t* **UXD Support**: \\_Not applicable — PQC is transparent to users; no UX changes\\_\n192\t* **Performance Team Support**: \\_Required — TLS handshake latency regression measurement needed before ship\\_\n193\t\n194\t## Staff Engineer / SME Input\n195\t\n196\t_Add technical corrections, architectural direction, component preferences, or domain expertise below. Write in declarative, cumulative form — statements that remain valid across refinement iterations. This input takes priority over architecture context when they conflict. After review: address findings, then remove the needs-attention label from Jira._\n197\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2ed9b966d085149","agent_id":"adb5edffc4ae3a249","session_id":"516e0302-0205-40f2-ace9-cb4df5d195a7","uuid":"62a13e5a-0a74-4ad8-91c9-93a93d9f07db","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:48:22.042Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-900eb383fe3fc4bc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8001"}
