{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a9768d65-a49e-4aaf-a8a1-3112cf722565","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-af8c0e9ff590e802","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8001-gx63tfdy/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8001-E002.md","content":"# PQC TLS handshake performance threshold and regression validation\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic closes the performance gap flagged in the strategy's Non-Functional\nRequirements: no numeric TLS handshake latency regression threshold exists (\"NEEDS DOMAIN\nINPUT\", owner: PM + Performance team lead, to be resolved before Sprint 2 ends), and a\nlatency regression measurement is required before RHOAI 3.6 ships. The epic ratifies the\nthreshold with the named owners and then measures the TLS handshake latency impact of the\nPQC hybrid key exchange (X25519MLKEM768) on PQC-switched runtime images against\nnon-PQC baselines, producing the pass/fail report the release gate needs.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Draft and ratify, with the PM and Performance team lead, a numeric TLS handshake\n  latency regression threshold for PQC-enabled runtime images, referencing the Chrome\n  ~10% maximum TLS handshake regression figure and the Red Hat PQC benchmarking outcome\n  (PSASTRAT-7170) as inputs. (This is the strategy's open performance decision; the\n  measurement methodology does not depend on the specific value.)\n- Measure TLS handshake latency for representative PQC-switched images (system-OpenSSL\n  components on `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` / `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`) versus their current\n  non-PQC baselines, covering both PQC-supporting clients and legacy non-PQC clients\n  (opportunistic hybrid negotiation must not regress classic ECDHE handshakes beyond the\n  threshold).\n- Produce a benchmark report: measured regressions per image class, pass/fail against\n  the ratified threshold, and — where the threshold is exceeded — a findings section\n  identifying the component and the remediation owner (the component team owning that\n  image), since remediation work is owned per component, not by this strategy.\n- Confirm the availability characteristic is preserved: the measurement setup must not\n  introduce HA-relevant differences (no additional replicas implied by the base image\n  switch).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A numeric TLS handshake latency regression threshold is documented and ratified by the\n  PM and the Performance team lead (the strategy's named owners for this decision).\n- Given PQC-switched runtime images, when TLS handshakes are measured against non-PQC\n  baselines with both PQC-capable and classic-ECDHE-only clients, then the measured\n  regression for every tested image class is reported and compared against the ratified\n  threshold, with a pass/fail verdict per class.\n- The final report is delivered before RHOAI 3.6 ship, and every image class exceeding\n  the threshold has an identified owning team and a recorded remediation handoff.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image for RHOAI 3.6 /\n  OCP 5.0** — this epic supplies the performance validation (Non-Functional Requirement:\n  Performance) that gates shipping the PQC-switched payload; the HLR's switch work itself\n  is owned by component teams and tracked via the compliance matrix.\n- Supporting: Non-Functional Requirements — Performance (NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT threshold,\n  owner PM + Performance team lead) and Backwards compatibility (classic ECDHE clients\n  must continue to work without exceeding the regression threshold).\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":38050,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b2ed9b966d085149","agent_id":"adb5edffc4ae3a249","session_id":"516e0302-0205-40f2-ace9-cb4df5d195a7","uuid":"924cb99e-bfe3-4287-b640-f86e9da289ef