{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b81c7113c8fc4cb3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8001-E003\n3\ttitle: Payload-wide PQC base image compliance verification and final accounting\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n5\tcomponent: CI/CD\n6\tteam: DevOps/Build\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P0\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-8001-E001\n11\tai_signals:\n12\t  change_specificity: 1\n13\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n14\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n15\t  existing_foundation: 1\n16\t  open_questions: 0\n17\t  external_dependency: 0\n18\t  human_process_gates: 0\n19\t  repo_access: 1\n20\t  architecture_claims: 1\n21\timplementation_type: null\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 4\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Payload-wide PQC base image compliance verification and final accounting\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tThis epic delivers the release-gating verification for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 PQC\n35\tcompliance: an automated scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload proving that switched runtime\n36\timages activate the `DEFAULT:PQ` crypto policy with ML-KEM algorithms available, and the\n37\tfinal compliance accounting showing that every RHOAI component image is either rebuilt on\n38\ta PQC-enabled base image or covered by a filed and acknowledged OCP exception — zero\n39\tunaccounted-for images. The verification runs against the image inventory and exception\n40\tledger maintained by the readiness matrix and compliance tracking work.\n41\t\n42\t## Scope\n43\t\n44\t- Implement or wire up the payload-wide compliance scan that executes\n45\t  `update-crypto-policies --show` and `openssl list -kem-algorithms` inside the runtime\n46\t  containers of the RHOAI 3.6 payload and records the results per image. The companion\n47\t  strategy RHAISTRAT-7816 owns TLS-scanner CI; if its scanner covers the crypto-policy\n48\t  check, integrate it — otherwise implement a dedicated crypto-policy scan (boundary to\n49\t  be confirmed, see decomposition health warnings).\n50\t- Apply the scan's applicability rules from the strategy: OpenSSL-backed components only\n51\t  (Go operators, Python components, native-tls Rust components); components on\n52\t  non-OpenSSL TLS stacks (rustls+ring, Java/NSS) are covered by OCP exception filings,\n53\t  not by the scan; on FIPS-mode clusters, FIPS policy deactivates PQC for this release —\n54\t  `DEFAULT:PQ` being inactive there is expected and is not a compliance failure.\n55\t- Produce the final compliance accounting for the September 15, 2026 deadline: for every\n56\t  image in the payload inventory, either (a) scan evidence of `DEFAULT:PQ` with\n57\t  ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024 present, or (b) a filed and acknowledged OCP\n58\t  exception with a remediation plan naming the component's TLS stack and why it cannot\n59\t  negotiate ML-KEM. The accounting terminates with zero unaccounted-for images.\n60\t- Report residual gaps (images neither switched nor exception-covered) with owning teams\n61\t  for escalation.\n62\t\n63\t## Acceptance Criteria\n64\t\n65\t- Given a switched OpenSSL-backed runtime image in the RHOAI 3.6 payload, when\n66\t  `update-crypto-policies --show` is executed inside the container, then the active\n67\t  crypto policy reports `DEFAULT:PQ`, and when `openssl list -kem-algorithms` is\n68\t  executed, then ML-KEM-512, ML-KEM-768, and ML-KEM-1024 appear in the output —\n69\t  verified by the automated scan across the payload, with results recorded per image.\n70\t- The scan correctly excludes non-OpenSSL components (covered by exception filings) and\n71\t  treats FIPS-mode clusters as expected-behavior rather than compliance failure, per the\n72\t  strategy's acceptance criteria notes.\n73\t- Given the payload inventory and the exception ledger, when the final accounting is\n74\t  compiled, then every RHOAI component image is in exactly one of two states — scanned\n75\t  PQC-compliant, or covered by a filed and acknowledged OCP exception with remediation\n76\t  plan — and the count of unaccounted-for images is zero, with any residual gaps\n77\t  escalated with named owning teams.\n78\t- The accounting is reproducible: re-running the scan and accounting on the same payload\n79\t  produces the same verdict per image.\n80\t\n81\t## HLR Traceability\n82\t\n83\t- **[P0] All RHOAI runtime images must use a PQC-enabled base image for RHOAI 3.6 /\n84\t  OCP 5.0** — this epic verifies the end state across the payload (strategy Acceptance\n85\t  Criterion 1).\n86\t- **[P0] Teams unable to comply by September 15 must file an OCP exception with a\n87\t  remediation plan** — this epic produces the final switch-vs-exception accounting with\n88\t  zero unaccounted-for images (strategy Acceptance Criterion 2).\n89\t- **[P0] PQC enablement is runtime only** — the scan targets the runtime container's\n90\t  active crypto policy, validating the runtime-only enablement model end to end.\n91\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-beeaf32dc01bd251","agent_id":"a91d1782451bbf9b1","session_id":"516e0302-0205-40f2-ace9-cb4df5d195a7","uuid":"719edf72-a447-4563-a34b-b6cfae531e80","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:53:53.069Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b81c7113c8fc4cb3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
