{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a05e097d109153af","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8001 (Base image compliance — Post-quantum cryptography support)\n10\t\n11\t## Strategy Characterization\n12\t\n13\tRHAISTRAT-8001 is a **compliance coordination tracker**, not a code-change strategy. The\n14\tstrategy is explicit that the actual base-image switch work and the OCP exception filings are\n15\towned by each component team via their own tracking tickets linked to RHAISTRAT-8001\n16\t(\"Implementation work is owned by each component team\"; \"OCP exception filing for individual\n17\tteams — each team files their own\" is listed as out-of-scope). The strategy's own deliverables\n18\tare therefore coordination, tracking, validation, and reporting artifacts. The epics below\n19\tdecompose the work this strategy owns; the ~153 per-image switches are tracked through the\n20\taggregation mechanism E001 establishes, not as epics of this decomposition.\n21\t\n22\t**Triage (Step 0):** Effort is S, but the strategy affects all RHOAI component images\n23\t(many components) and names three distinct team groups (all component teams, AIPCC team,\n24\tDevOps/Build team, plus required Performance team support). The below-threshold single\n25\tepic path does not apply. Not documentation-only: the strategy coordinates rebuilds and\n26\tgates a release, and produces engineering artifacts (readiness matrix, verification,\n27\tperformance validation), not docs content.\n28\t\n29\t**Staff Engineer Input:** The section contains only template placeholder text — no\n30\toverrides to log.\n31\t\n32\t## Epic List\n33\t\n34\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n35\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n36\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 | PQC Readiness Matrix and compliance tracking for all RHOAI runtime images | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E002 | PQC TLS handshake performance threshold and regression validation | Implementation | Performance | P1 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E003 | Payload-wide PQC base image compliance verification and final accounting | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n39\t\n40\t## Dependency DAG\n41\t\n42\t```mermaid\n43\tgraph TD\n44\t    E001[\"E001 Readiness Matrix & compliance tracking\"]\n45\t    E002[\"E002 TLS handshake perf threshold & validation\"]\n46\t    E003[\"E003 Payload compliance verification & accounting\"]\n47\t    E001 --> E003\n48\t```\n49\t\n50\tE001 and E002 are parallel roots. E002 is deliberately not wired into E001 or E003: the\n51\tperformance threshold and handshake measurements neither produce an artifact the other\n52\tepics build against nor consume the readiness matrix — they validate different properties\n53\t(crypto policy presence vs. handshake latency) on independently available switched images.\n54\t\n55\t## DAG Justification\n56\t\n57\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n58\t|------|------|-----------|\n59\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The final compliance accounting (every image switched or exception-filed, zero unaccounted) is compiled against the readiness matrix inventory and the linked-ticket tracking state E001 produces. E003 would have no authoritative image list or exception ledger to verify against without it. |\n60\t| E002 ∥ E001, E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (independent, no shared artifacts) | Performance validation measures TLS handshake latency on PQC-switched images; it shares no data structure or API surface with the tracking matrix or the compliance scan. It produces an independent report consumed by release gating, not by E001/E003. |\n61\t| No Investigation epics | Step 3 decision rule | See \"Unknowns Evaluation\" below — every open question either resolves outside this strategy's epic set or does not change which epics exist. |\n62\t\n63\t## Unknowns Evaluation (Step 3)\n64\t\n65\t| Unknown (from strategy) | Changes which epics exist / what they do? | Handling |\n66\t|---|---|---|\n67\t| PQC variants of `ubi9/python-311`, `ubi9/python-312`, `ubi9/nodejs-22` (RHOAIENG-7195, AIPCC) | No — this strategy's epics (tracking, validation, verification) exist and behave identically either way; the outcome only determines whether ~10 affected teams switch bases or file their own OCP exceptions | External dependency with uncertain timing → tiered delivery (see below); tracked as a status column in E001's matrix and a risk/AC on E003's accounting. Not an epic here — AIPCC's spike is already tracked in RHOAIENG-7195 (Rule 21: third party resolves → precondition, no separate epic). |\n68\t| AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date (AIPCC-7258) | No — same reasoning; gates only whether teams on `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` can adopt or must file exceptions | External dependency; monitored via E001 tracking; health warning below. |\n69\t| Which teams file OCP exceptions vs. complete the switch | No — this is the subject matter E001 aggregates; each team self-identifies via their own linked tickets | Aggregated by E001; verified by E003 (AC: zero unaccounted-for images). |\n70\t| Performance NFR \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\" — no numeric TLS handshake regression threshold (owner: PM + Performance team lead, due before Sprint 2 ends) | No — E002 performs the same work (ratify threshold, measure, report) regardless of the value; a failed threshold triggers fix-and-retry remediation owned by component teams, not different epics | Explicitly flagged pending decision → captured as a mandatory AC on E002 (threshold ratified by PM + Performance team lead) and a health warning below. Not downgraded silently: the strategy flags it, and it stays visible as a gating AC with a named owner. |\n71\t\n72\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n73\t\n74\tStrategy HLRs (unnumbered in the strategy; labeled here for reference):\n75\t\n76\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n77\t|-----|----------|---------|\n78\t| HLR-1: All RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image (`ubi9-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, or equivalent) for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 | P0 | E001 (per-image target mapping and switch/exception status tracking), E003 (payload-wide verification that switched images report `DEFAULT:PQ` with ML-KEM algorithms) |\n79\t| HLR-2: Teams unable to comply by September 15, 2026 file an OCP exception with a remediation plan | P0 | E001 (exception ledger and deadline enforcement/escalation), E003 (final accounting: every image switched or exception filed, zero unaccounted) |\n80\t| HLR-3: PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage of the Dockerfile/Containerfile changes | P0 | E001 (runtime-stage-only switch guidance encoded as a column/instructions in the readiness matrix; E003's scan targets the runtime image) |\n81\t| HLR-4 (Deferred): PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | Out of scope by strategy decision — no epic, per strategy |\n82\t\n83\tNon-functional requirements coverage: the Security NFR (ML-KEM hybrid key exchange on\n84\tnon-Go components) is inherited by component-team switch work and verified by E003's\n85\tpayload scan; the Performance NFR (handshake regression threshold) is owned by E002;\n86\tBackwards compatibility and Availability NFRs are properties of the opportunistic hybrid\n87\tkey exchange and base-image swap, verified through E002/E003 measurement respectively.\n88\t\n89\t## Health Warnings\n90\t\n91\t1. **AC-1 measurement owner ambiguity (scope trap).** AC-1 is \"measured by automated CI\n92\t   scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\", but TLS-scanner CI is explicitly owned by the\n93\t   companion strategy RHAISTRAT-7816 and is out of scope here. It is unclear whether a\n94\t   crypto-policy scan (`update-crypto-policies --show` / `openssl list -kem-algorithms`\n95\t   inside payload containers) exists or must be built. E003's scope allows either\n96\t   (leverage the companion strategy's scanner or implement a payload crypto-policy scan);\n97\t   the boundary with RHAISTRAT-7816 should be confirmed by a human.\n98\t2. **External dependency timing.** RHOAIENG-7195 (PQC python/nodejs base variants) and\n99\t   AIPCC-7258 (RHEL 9.8 rebase gating `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` adoption) have\n100\t   unconfirmed dates. Teams on those bases (~10 images for python/nodejs, ~34 for\n101\t   rhel9-8-els) may be forced into OCP exceptions. Mitigation is the strategy's own:\n102\t   escalate if no committed date by early September.\n103\t3. **Unresolved performance threshold.** The performance NFR is flagged NEEDS DOMAIN\n104\t   INPUT with no numeric regression threshold (owner: PM + Performance team lead; due\n105\t   before Sprint 2 ends). E002 cannot produce a pass/fail report until it is ratified.\n106\t4. **Deadline risk is a schedule constraint, not an AC.** September 15, 2026 and the\n107\t   externally-gated dependencies are tracked as risks; E003's AC is written at the\n108\t   engineering completion point under this strategy's control (the accounting exists and\n109\t   is accurate), not on external team delivery timing.\n110\t5. **Section shape.** Affected Components and Impacted Teams are prose/bullets rather\n111\t   than tables; parsed as described above. No information was lost, but the strategy\n112\t   deviates from template shape.\n113\t\n114\t## Tiered Delivery\n115\t\n116\tApplicable as a **tracking-tier distinction**, not as epic splits, because the gated work\n117\t(switching python/nodejs/rhel9-8-els-based images) is owned by component teams, not by\n118\tthis strategy's epics:\n119\t\n120\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, available now):** ~68 `ubi9/ubi-minimal`-based images can\n121\t  switch to `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` (confirmed available, OCPSTRAT-7413). E001 tracking,\n122\t  E002 performance validation, and E003 verification of switched images all proceed.\n123\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** ~34 `rhel9-6-els`-based images (gated by AIPCC-7258\n124\t  RHEL 9.8 rebase) and ~10 python/nodejs-based images (gated by RHOAIENG-7195 PQC\n125\t  variants). Fallback per the strategy: affected teams file OCP exceptions with\n126\t  remediation plans by September 15. E001's matrix carries a per-image tier/status\n127\t  column; E003's accounting accepts either terminal state (switched or exception-filed).\n128\t\n129\t## Component Mapping Notes\n130\t\n131\t- E001 → `Build and Release`: the strategy assigns the DevOps/Build team \"coordinate base\n132\t  image pipeline updates\", and the source inventory (RHOAI-Build-Config CSV, organized\n133\t  image list) lives in build-config territory.\n134\t- E002 → `PerfScale`: performance validation work owned by the Performance team\n135\t  (Performance Team Support: Required).\n136\t- E003 → `CI/CD`: payload-wide automated compliance scanning is CI work; same owning\n137\t  DevOps/Build team as E001 but a distinct work surface (automated verification vs.\n138\t  tracking artifact), so it is a separate epic per the epic boundary rules.\n139\t- No component in this decomposition is new or absent from architecture context\n140\t  (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/` contains the referenced\n141\t  PLATFORM.md and fms-guardrails-orchestrator.md), so no onboarding chain is generated.\n142\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8d0809db81548801","agent_id":"a4d18f23e96dcae9b","session_id":"516e0302-0205-40f2-ace9-cb4df5d195a7","uuid":"3819076a-37e2-4442-a4f3-cc2440091efc","timestamp":"2026-10-22T04:58:13.156Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a05e097d109153af","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Revise RHAISTRAT-8001 decomposition"}
