{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6dc8e25b48e2518","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8001\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8001 (Base image compliance — Post-quantum cryptography support)\n11\t\n12\t## Strategy Characterization\n13\t\n14\tRHAISTRAT-8001 is a **compliance coordination tracker**, not a code-change strategy. The\n15\tstrategy is explicit that the actual base-image switch work and the OCP exception filings are\n16\towned by each component team via their own tracking tickets linked to RHAISTRAT-8001\n17\t(\"Implementation work is owned by each component team\"; \"OCP exception filing for individual\n18\tteams — each team files their own\" is listed as out-of-scope). The strategy's own deliverables\n19\tare therefore coordination, tracking, validation, and reporting artifacts. The epics below\n20\tdecompose the work this strategy owns; the ~153 per-image switches are tracked through the\n21\taggregation mechanism E001 establishes, not as epics of this decomposition.\n22\t\n23\t**Triage (Step 0):** Effort is S, but the strategy affects all RHOAI component images\n24\t(many components) and names three distinct team groups (all component teams, AIPCC team,\n25\tDevOps/Build team, plus required Performance team support). The below-threshold single\n26\tepic path does not apply. Not documentation-only: the strategy coordinates rebuilds and\n27\tgates a release, and produces engineering artifacts (readiness matrix, verification,\n28\tperformance validation), not docs content.\n29\t\n30\t**Staff Engineer Input:** The section contains only template placeholder text — no\n31\toverrides to log.\n32\t\n33\t## Epic List\n34\t\n35\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n36\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n37\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E001 | PQC Readiness Matrix and compliance tracking for all RHOAI runtime images | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n38\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E002 | PQC TLS handshake performance threshold and regression validation | Implementation | Performance | P1 |\n39\t| RHAISTRAT-8001-E003 | Payload-wide PQC base image compliance verification and final accounting | Implementation | DevOps/Build | P0 |\n40\t\n41\t## Dependency DAG\n42\t\n43\t```mermaid\n44\tgraph TD\n45\t    E001[\"E001 Readiness Matrix & compliance tracking\"]\n46\t    E002[\"E002 TLS handshake perf threshold & validation\"]\n47\t    E003[\"E003 Payload compliance verification & accounting\"]\n48\t    E001 --> E003\n49\t```\n50\t\n51\tE001 and E002 are parallel roots. E002 is deliberately not wired into E001 or E003: the\n52\tperformance threshold and handshake measurements neither produce an artifact the other\n53\tepics build against nor consume the readiness matrix — they validate different properties\n54\t(crypto policy presence vs. handshake latency) on independently available switched images.\n55\t\n56\t## DAG Justification\n57\t\n58\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n59\t|------|------|-----------|\n60\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 13 (artifact consumer) | The final compliance accounting (every image switched or exception-filed, zero unaccounted) is compiled against the readiness matrix inventory and the linked-ticket tracking state E001 produces. E003 would have no authoritative image list or exception ledger to verify against without it. |\n61\t| E002 ∥ E001, E002 ∥ E003 | Rule 14 (independent, no shared artifacts) | Performance validation measures TLS handshake latency on PQC-switched images; it shares no data structure or API surface with the tracking matrix or the compliance scan. Its measurement targets are selected from the strategy's published base image mapping and the RHOAI 3.5 relatedImages inventory — not from the readiness matrix — so it consumes no artifact the other epics produce. It produces an independent report consumed by release gating, not by E001/E003. |\n62\t| No Investigation epics | Step 3 decision rule | See \"Unknowns Evaluation\" below — every open question either resolves outside this strategy's epic set or does not change which epics exist. |\n63\t\n64\t## Unknowns Evaluation (Step 3)\n65\t\n66\t| Unknown (from strategy) | Changes which epics exist / what they do? | Handling |\n67\t|---|---|---|\n68\t| PQC variants of `ubi9/python-311`, `ubi9/python-312`, `ubi9/nodejs-22` (RHOAIENG-7195, AIPCC) | No — this strategy's epics (tracking, validation, verification) exist and behave identically either way; the outcome only determines whether ~10 affected teams switch bases or file their own OCP exceptions | External dependency with uncertain timing → tiered delivery (see below); tracked as a status column in E001's matrix and a risk/AC on E003's accounting. Not an epic here — AIPCC's spike is already tracked in RHOAIENG-7195 (Rule 21: third party resolves → precondition, no separate epic). |\n69\t| AIPCC RHEL 9.8 rebase committed date (AIPCC-7258) | No — same reasoning; gates only whether teams on `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` can adopt or must file exceptions | External dependency; monitored via E001 tracking; health warning below. |\n70\t| Which teams file OCP exceptions vs. complete the switch | No — this is the subject matter E001 aggregates; each team self-identifies via their own linked tickets | Aggregated by E001; verified by E003 (AC: zero unaccounted-for images). |\n71\t| Performance NFR \"NEEDS DOMAIN INPUT\" — no numeric TLS handshake regression threshold (owner: PM + Performance team lead, due before Sprint 2 ends) | No — E002 performs the same work (ratify threshold, measure, report) regardless of the value; a failed threshold triggers fix-and-retry remediation owned by component teams, not different epics | Explicitly flagged pending decision → captured as a mandatory AC on E002 (threshold ratified by PM + Performance team lead) and a health warning below. Not downgraded silently: the strategy flags it, and it stays visible as a gating AC with a named owner. |\n72\t\n73\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n74\t\n75\tStrategy HLRs (unnumbered in the strategy; labeled here for reference):\n76\t\n77\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n78\t|-----|----------|---------|\n79\t| HLR-1: All RHOAI runtime images use a PQC-enabled base image (`ubi9-minimal-pqc`, `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal`, or equivalent) for RHOAI 3.6 / OCP 5.0 | P0 | E001 (per-image target mapping and switch/exception status tracking), E003 (payload-wide verification that switched images report `DEFAULT:PQ` with ML-KEM algorithms); supporting trace: E002 (performance NFR validation of the switched payload — validates, does not deliver, the HLR) |\n80\t| HLR-2: Teams unable to comply by September 15, 2026 file an OCP exception with a remediation plan | P0 | E001 (exception ledger and deadline enforcement/escalation), E003 (final accounting: every image switched or exception filed, zero unaccounted) |\n81\t| HLR-3: PQC enablement is runtime only — only the final stage of the Dockerfile/Containerfile changes | P0 | E001 (runtime-stage-only switch guidance encoded as a column/instructions in the readiness matrix; E003's scan targets the runtime image) |\n82\t| HLR-4 (Deferred): PQC-signed X.509 certificates (ML-DSA) | Deferred | Out of scope by strategy decision — no epic, per strategy |\n83\t\n84\tNon-functional requirements coverage: the Security NFR (ML-KEM hybrid key exchange on\n85\tnon-Go components) is inherited by component-team switch work and verified by E003's\n86\tpayload scan; the Performance NFR (handshake regression threshold) is owned by E002;\n87\tBackwards compatibility and Availability NFRs are properties of the opportunistic hybrid\n88\tkey exchange and base-image swap, verified through E002/E003 measurement respectively.\n89\t\n90\t## Health Warnings\n91\t\n92\t1. **AC-1 measurement owner ambiguity (scope trap).** AC-1 is \"measured by automated CI\n93\t   scan across the RHOAI 3.6 payload\", but TLS-scanner CI is explicitly owned by the\n94\t   companion strategy RHAISTRAT-7816 and is out of scope here. It is unclear whether a\n95\t   crypto-policy scan (`update-crypto-policies --show` / `openssl list -kem-algorithms`\n96\t   inside payload containers) exists or must be built. E003's scope allows either\n97\t   (leverage the companion strategy's scanner or implement a payload crypto-policy scan);\n98\t   the boundary with RHAISTRAT-7816 should be confirmed by a human.\n99\t2. **External dependency timing.** RHOAIENG-7195 (PQC python/nodejs base variants) and\n100\t   AIPCC-7258 (RHEL 9.8 rebase gating `rhel9-8-els/pqc-minimal` adoption) have\n101\t   unconfirmed dates. Teams on those bases (~10 images for python/nodejs, ~34 for\n102\t   rhel9-8-els) may be forced into OCP exceptions. Mitigation is the strategy's own:\n103\t   escalate if no committed date by early September.\n104\t3. **Unresolved performance threshold.** The performance NFR is flagged NEEDS DOMAIN\n105\t   INPUT with no numeric regression threshold (owner: PM + Performance team lead; due\n106\t   before Sprint 2 ends). E002 cannot produce a pass/fail report until it is ratified.\n107\t4. **Deadline risk is a schedule constraint, not an AC.** September 15, 2026 and the\n108\t   externally-gated dependencies are tracked as risks; E003's AC is written at the\n109\t   engineering completion point under this strategy's control (the accounting exists and\n110\t   is accurate), not on external team delivery timing.\n111\t5. **Section shape.** Affected Components and Impacted Teams are prose/bullets rather\n112\t   than tables; parsed as described above. No information was lost, but the strategy\n113\t   deviates from template shape.\n114\t6. **Documentation Support gate is pending, not confirmed.** The strategy's\n115\t   Prerequisites list \"Documentation Support: Pending human review\", so Rule 11 does\n116\t   not trigger and no docs-authoring epic is generated. If that gate is later confirmed\n117\t   as \"Yes\", a docs-authoring epic blocked by the implementation epics (E001–E003)\n118\t   must be added to this decomposition.\n119\t\n120\t## Tiered Delivery\n121\t\n122\tApplicable as a **tracking-tier distinction**, not as epic splits, because the gated work\n123\t(switching python/nodejs/rhel9-8-els-based images) is owned by component teams, not by\n124\tthis strategy's epics:\n125\t\n126\t- **Tier 1 (independent value, available now):** ~68 `ubi9/ubi-minimal`-based images can\n127\t  switch to `ubi9/ubi-minimal-pqc` (confirmed available, OCPSTRAT-7413). E001 tracking,\n128\t  E002 performance validation, and E003 verification of switched images all proceed.\n129\t- **Tier 2 (dependency-gated):** ~34 `rhel9-6-els`-based images (gated by AIPCC-7258\n130\t  RHEL 9.8 rebase) and ~10 python/nodejs-based images (gated by RHOAIENG-7195 PQC\n131\t  variants). Fallback per the strategy: affected teams file OCP exceptions with\n132\t  remediation plans by September 15. E001's matrix carries a per-image tier/status\n133\t  column; E003's accounting accepts either terminal state (switched or exception-filed).\n134\t\n135\t## Component Mapping Notes\n136\t\n137\t- E001 → `Build and Release`: the strategy assigns the DevOps/Build team \"coordinate base\n138\t  image pipeline updates\", and the source inventory (RHOAI-Build-Config CSV, organized\n139\t  image list) lives in build-config territory.\n140\t- E002 → `PerfScale`: performance validation work owned by the Performance team\n141\t  (Performance Team Support: Required).\n142\t- E003 → `CI/CD`: payload-wide automated compliance scanning is CI work; same owning\n143\t  DevOps/Build team as E001 but a distinct work surface (automated verification vs.\n144\t  tracking artifact), so it is a separate epic per the epic boundary rules.\n145\t- No component in this decomposition is new or absent from architecture context\n146\t  (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/` contains the referenced\n147\t  PLATFORM.md and fms-guardrails-orchestrator.md), so no onboarding chain is generated.\n148\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9793ed84e6c2a765","agent_id":"adfaf49687e6ebcf6","session_id":"516e0302-0205-40f2-ace9-cb4df5d195a7","uuid":"ea2d0b6b-92a5-451b-b892-42fe34c29787","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:00:20.398Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a6dc8e25b48e2518","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8001"}
